    The linuxtv-ext-header defines tv extensions not defined in the standard
or de facto standard. There are two directories at this moment: 'dvbv5-ext'
and 'v4l2-ext'. The header files in this repository are used for implementing
both kernel and user drivers. Developers should not define data structures
or types that are used exclusively in userland.


========================================
  DIRECTORIES
========================================
    Currently, there are two directories extending standards, 'dvbv5-ext',
'v4l2-ext'. If you need to extend another standard, you can add a directory,
e.g., 'alsa-ext' directory.

linux/dvbv5-ext:
    This directory defines tv extensions not defined in the dvbv5. Every
header in this directory should include the 'linuxtv-ext-ver.h' like below.

    #include <linux/linuxtv-ext-ver.h>

linux/v4l2-ext:
    This directory defines tv extensions not defined in the v4l2. Every
header in this directory should include 'linuxtv-ext-ver.h' like below.

    #include <linux/linuxtv-ext-ver.h>


========================================
  VERSION
========================================
    Platform software can run on two different soc drivers. In the
development phase, each driver implementation may not be able to keep the
same header version for each other. For example, some APIs implemented in
soc A is not yet implemented in soc B. To prevent inconvenience caused by
this, userland developer can use the following version:

    #if (LINUXTV_EXT_VER_CUR > LINUXTV_EXT_VER(1,0,0,1)
        // do something that you can afeter v1.0.0-1
    #else
        // do something that you can before v1.0.0-1
    #endif

    The version is composed of MAJOR, MINOR, PATCH, and SUBMISSION defined
in the linuxtv-ext-ver.h. Every header defining APIs or data structure
shared by both kernel and userspace should include 'linuxtv-ext-ver.h' file.
SUBMISSION should be identical with the tag submission. Therefore API
manager should set SUBMISSION field in 'linuxtv-ext-ver.h' before he or she
tags a submission.


========================================
  Notes for userland software developer
========================================
    Headers under dvbv5-ext will be placed under
'/sysroot/usr/include/linux/dvbv5-ext/' directory. In oe-build,
'linuxtv-ext-header.bb' file do this for you. You can include the header
as below.

    #include <linux/dvbv5-ext/someheader.h>


========================================
  Notes for kernel driver developer
========================================
    Kernel driver developers should put headers in this repository on proper
location on their own. In the oe-build environment, you can add dependency
to 'linuxtv-ext-header'. Before fetching your driver, you should copy
'linux/dvbv5-ext/*', 'linux/v4l2-ext/*', and 'linux/linuxtv-ext-ver.h' to place where you want
, probably under $kernel_src/include/uapi/linux.
